If I have a collection of
objects that I can add and

00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:21.160
scalar multiply, what is the
term that I would use for the

00:13:21.160 --> 00:13:24.540
general operation I can do to
these elements.

00:13:24.540 --> 00:13:27.660
If I can add them, and I can do
scalar multiplication,

00:13:27.660 --> 00:13:29.980
what would I say that these
objects can do in general?

00:13:29.980 --> 00:13:32.140
Jobendy? Linear combinations?
Yeah, they support linear

00:13:32.140 --> 00:13:33.140
combinations.

00:13:33.460 --> 00:13:37.400
So elements of a vector space

00:13:37.400 --> 00:13:42.820
support linear combinations.

00:13:48.300 --> 00:13:49.300
OK?

00:13:51.300 --> 00:13:53.560
Now you may say this seems a
little redundant.

00:13:53.560 --> 00:13:55.980
Like you've already told me that
I can add and scale or multiply

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:57.180
them.
It's like what is really new

00:13:57.180 --> 00:13:58.680
about this?

00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:01.300
The new thing, and we're not
going to spend a ton of time

00:14:01.300 --> 00:14:03.440
talking about this, but it's
like important for you to know,

00:14:03.440 --> 00:14:06.620
is that vector spaces don't need
to be made up of vectors in the

00:14:06.620 --> 00:14:09.380
way that we've seen them before.

00:14:09.380 --> 00:14:11.260
Here's a vector space that
you've probably never thought

00:14:11.260 --> 00:14:12.760
about before.

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:14.820
The set of polynomials

00:14:14.820 --> 00:14:19.980
with degree,

00:14:20.260 --> 00:14:22.220
let's say,

00:14:22.500 --> 00:14:24.750
equal to 3.

00:14:26.800 --> 00:14:29.660
So again, we're not looking at
vectors the way that you've

00:14:29.660 --> 00:14:31.060
thought about them before.
We're thinking about functions

00:14:31.060 --> 00:14:32.060
now.

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:34.400
But it turns out that there are
vector spaces made up of

00:14:34.400 --> 00:14:35.400
functions.

00:14:37.580 --> 00:14:41.740
So is the set of polynomials
with degree equal to 3 a vector

00:14:41.740 --> 00:14:44.380
space? What would that mean? It
would have to mean that you can

00:14:44.380 --> 00:14:47.440
take two elements of the set.

00:14:47.440 --> 00:14:49.420
But here, the elements are
functions,

00:14:49.420 --> 00:14:51.880
not vectors, that I would write
as little lists.

00:14:51.880 --> 00:14:54.060
But they'd be functions.

00:14:54.060 --> 00:14:58.380
And it needs to be the case that
if I take any linear combination

00:14:58.380 --> 00:15:00.860
of these functions, like
something times u of x plus

00:15:00.860 --> 00:15:02.260
something times v of x.

00:15:02.260 --> 00:15:05.040
Don't need to call them u and v.
I'm just using that to mimic the

00:15:05.040 --> 00:15:07.060
notation on the last slide.
I could have called this f and

00:15:07.060 --> 00:15:08.060
g.

00:15:08.520 --> 00:15:11.540
It needs to be the case that if
I multiply the first function by

00:15:11.540 --> 00:15:14.700
2 and the second function by 7
and add the results,

00:15:14.700 --> 00:15:17.240
the new function 2u plus 7v

00:15:17.240 --> 00:15:20.540
is also in the set.

00:15:20.540 --> 00:15:22.140
So let's make up an example.

00:15:22.140 --> 00:15:27.390
2x cubed minus x squared plus 7x
minus 9,

00:15:27.800 --> 00:15:31.550
and x cubed plus 4.

00:15:31.720 --> 00:15:35.380
These are two elements of the
set of polynomials with degree

00:15:35.380 --> 00:15:37.340
equal to 3.

00:15:37.340 --> 00:15:39.240
If I add them,

00:15:39.240 --> 00:15:42.040
do I get a new polynomial with
degree 3?

00:15:42.040 --> 00:15:42.700
Sure.

00:15:42.700 --> 00:15:45.600
If I multiply them by scalars,
do I also have polynomials with

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:46.720
degree 3?

00:15:46.720 --> 00:15:47.720
Sure.

00:15:47.780 --> 00:15:50.700
But you should see that there's
something slightly wrong about

00:15:50.700 --> 00:15:53.320
the definition of the vector
space I wrote on the page.

00:15:53.320 --> 00:15:56.320
And why is that?

00:15:58.140 --> 00:15:58.940
Got it.

00:15:58.940 --> 00:16:02.800
What if the x had a negative 2x
cubed?

00:16:02.800 --> 00:16:03.260
Good.

00:16:03.260 --> 00:16:05.860
And if you added them, you would
no longer have a polynomial to

00:16:05.860 --> 00:16:06.900
be equal to.
Exactly.

00:16:06.900 --> 00:16:10.820
Or what if I did u of x minus 2
times v of x, which is the same

00:16:10.820 --> 00:16:12.820
thing as what you're saying,
right?

00:16:12.820 --> 00:16:16.860
What I just said two slides ago
was that a vector space is just

00:16:16.860 --> 00:16:19.800
a collection of things that if
you take any linear combination

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:20.660
of them,

00:16:20.660 --> 00:16:23.920
they still obey the properties
of the collection.

00:16:23.920 --> 00:16:24.580
But here,

00:16:24.580 --> 00:16:29.300
do you agree that if I took 1
times the first function minus 2

00:16:29.300 --> 00:16:32.300
times the second function and
looked at what that new function

00:16:32.300 --> 00:16:35.180
would be, it wouldn't have an x
cubed term.

00:16:35.180 --> 00:16:39.780
That 2x cubed would subtract out
with a negative 2 times x cubed

00:16:39.780 --> 00:16:44.240
in the second, and it wouldn't
be a polynomial of degree 3.

00:16:44.240 --> 00:16:48.320
So as I've written it, v is not
a vector space.

00:16:48.320 --> 00:16:52.600
But we could change it to be by
just changing this requirement

00:16:52.600 --> 00:16:58.600
to be less than or equal to 3.

00:16:58.840 --> 00:17:00.880
And that is a perfectly valid
vector space.

00:17:00.880 --> 00:17:06.580
If you take two functions or two
polynomials who have a degree

00:17:06.580 --> 00:17:08.040
less than or equal to 3

00:17:08.040 --> 00:17:10.880
and take any linear combination
of them you want,

00:17:10.880 --> 00:17:13.520
you're also going to get back a
new polynomial whose degree is

00:17:13.520 --> 00:17:14.960
less than or equal to 3.

00:17:14.960 --> 00:17:18.620
And that's what makes these
polynomials a vector space.

00:17:18.620 --> 00:17:21.260
So this is an abstract idea.

00:17:21.260 --> 00:17:23.420
And it's not one that we're
really going to spend that much

00:17:23.420 --> 00:17:25.080
time on in this class.

00:17:25.080 --> 00:17:28.340
But it comes up often enough in
machine learning that you just

00:17:28.340 --> 00:17:30.640
need to know about it, that you
will hear about vector spaces

00:17:30.640 --> 00:17:32.560
that are made up of things that
don't look like vectors that

00:17:32.560 --> 00:17:33.400
you've seen.

00:17:33.400 --> 00:17:37.300
And all that really means is
that whatever those objects are,

00:17:37.300 --> 00:17:40.740
whether in the future they're
matrices or some other more

00:17:40.740 --> 00:17:42.140
complicated thing,

00:17:42.140 --> 00:17:44.800
all that means is that these
objects, you can take linear

00:17:44.800 --> 00:17:47.800
combinations of them and they
still give you back objects in

00:17:47.800 --> 00:17:49.300
the set.

00:17:49.420 --> 00:17:49.920
Paul.

00:17:49.920 --> 00:17:54.860
Are you finding why f and v have
to be less than 3

00:17:54.860 --> 00:17:56.960
but not greater?

00:17:56.960 --> 00:18:00.100
Well, if you add two polynomial,
If you add two parabolas, you

00:18:00.100 --> 00:18:03.600
don't get something that's more
higher degree than a parabola.

00:18:03.600 --> 00:18:05.320
Same kind of idea.

00:18:05.320 --> 00:18:08.820
But if you did x squared plus
negative x squared,

00:18:08.820 --> 00:18:11.660
it could no longer be a
parabola.

00:18:11.660 --> 00:18:12.400
Yeah,

00:18:12.400 --> 00:18:13.400
good.

00:18:26.620 --> 00:18:27.440
OK,

00:18:27.440 --> 00:18:29.460
are we convinced that this is a
vector space? It's sort of

00:18:29.460 --> 00:18:32.020
abstract, but it's like a
collection of things that you

00:18:32.020 --> 00:18:34.080
can take linear combinations of.

00:18:34.080 --> 00:18:35.360
We agree?

00:18:35.360 --> 00:18:36.360
OK.

00:18:38.420 --> 00:18:41.680
Now, I should say the vector
spaces that we normally work

00:18:41.680 --> 00:18:47.060
with we always work

00:18:47.060 --> 00:18:52.940
with Rn where n depending on the
situation is replaced with a

00:18:52.940 --> 00:18:56.220
specific number like two or
three but Rn is an example of a

00:18:56.220 --> 00:18:58.960
vector space okay it's a
collection of vectors that can

00:18:58.960 --> 00:19:01.060
be added in scalar and multiply,

00:19:01.060 --> 00:19:02.060
OK?

00:19:04.440 --> 00:19:09.560
And vectors in Rn

00:19:09.560 --> 00:19:13.620
are sometimes called Euclidean
vectors.

00:19:19.160 --> 00:19:23.660
Does anyone know where the word
Euclidean comes from?

00:19:29.280 --> 00:19:32.940
Yeah, ancient Greek
mathematician invented a lot of

00:19:32.940 --> 00:19:36.780
ideas or thought about a lot of
ideas in geometry and that sort

00:19:36.780 --> 00:19:39.380
of corresponds to the fact that
we think of the vectors that

00:19:39.380 --> 00:19:42.400
we've seen in our class so far
as being objects in geometry

00:19:42.400 --> 00:19:44.980
that have a length and a
direction and we can create

00:19:44.980 --> 00:19:46.660
other geometric structures from
them.

00:19:46.660 --> 00:19:47.540
Okay,

00:19:47.540 --> 00:19:50.360
my point is that vector spaces
could be made up of things that

00:19:50.360 --> 00:19:53.300
don't have geometry like
polynomials Okay,

00:19:53.300 --> 00:19:55.840
if you're curious you can look
into the idea of like what is

00:19:55.840 --> 00:19:59.020
the dot product between two
polynomials?

00:19:59.020 --> 00:19:59.280
Okay,

00:19:59.280 --> 00:20:01.900
someone had to think about that,
right? Like what if you wanted

00:20:01.900 --> 00:20:03.920
to come up with vectors that
didn't look like lists of

00:20:03.920 --> 00:20:06.340
numbers and you wanted to take
the dot Product between them.

00:20:06.340 --> 00:20:07.400
What would that be?

00:20:07.400 --> 00:20:09.460
Turns out it involves
integrating the product of the

00:20:09.460 --> 00:20:11.700
function Okay, and that involves
calculus now We're not going to

00:20:11.700 --> 00:20:13.440
talk about that, but I'm just
telling you that there's a whole

00:20:13.440 --> 00:20:16.180
other world of stuff like this
out there

00:20:16.180 --> 00:20:18.600
So that's what a vector space
is, but that's not a term we

00:20:18.600 --> 00:20:20.640
really used before today.

00:20:20.640 --> 00:20:25.140
The term we used before today
was that of a subspace.

00:20:25.460 --> 00:20:31.320
Now the picture you should have
in your mind is that

00:20:31.320 --> 00:20:37.240
a subspace is itself a vector
space contained within

00:20:37.240 --> 00:20:40.240
a bigger vector space.

00:20:45.480 --> 00:20:50.730
So suppose v is a vector space.

00:20:54.080 --> 00:20:58.720
All that means, remember, is
that v is a set of objects that

00:20:58.720 --> 00:21:03.970
will allow you to add and scalar
multiply those objects.

00:21:06.340 --> 00:21:11.780
And that S is a subset

00:21:11.780 --> 00:21:14.420
of V.

00:21:16.940 --> 00:21:22.080
You can come up with lots of
subsets that are not subspaces.

00:21:22.080 --> 00:21:25.100
Subspace is a different word
than subset, and we'll look at

00:21:25.100 --> 00:21:27.920
some examples that clarify this
in just a bit.

00:21:27.920 --> 00:21:33.860
S is a subspace of

00:21:33.860 --> 00:21:35.480
V if,

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:41.440
what do you think makes a subset
a subspace, if you had to guess,

00:21:41.440 --> 00:21:45.940
based on the picture that I've
drawn?

00:21:46.000 --> 00:21:49.820
Say V is a big collection of
objects.

00:21:49.820 --> 00:21:51.760
Say it's a collection of
vectors,

00:21:51.760 --> 00:21:53.740
vectors in R2 if you will.

00:21:53.740 --> 00:21:56.580
I can make up any subset of
vectors in R2 if I want.

00:21:56.580 --> 00:21:57.240
I could say,

00:21:57.240 --> 00:22:00.800
think of all the vectors in R2
whose first component is 17.

00:22:00.800 --> 00:22:03.160
That's a subset of vectors in
R2.

00:22:03.160 --> 00:22:04.880
But it's not a subspace.

00:22:04.880 --> 00:22:09.060
What do you think a subspace
must satisfy?

00:22:09.060 --> 00:22:09.680
Yeah?

00:22:09.680 --> 00:22:13.000
Do subspaces have to be proper
subsets of V?

00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:17.260
Do they have to be all of V, or
does it have to be like? It can

00:22:17.260 --> 00:22:18.280
be all of V.

00:22:18.280 --> 00:22:23.360
So R2 is a subspace of itself,
for example.

00:22:23.360 --> 00:22:24.860
Zara? Well,

00:22:24.880 --> 00:22:29.380
every element in the subspace is
also a subset.

00:22:29.400 --> 00:22:32.340
Every element in the subspace is
also a V.

00:22:32.340 --> 00:22:35.140
That is true, but that's true
from the fact that I'm saying it

00:22:35.140 --> 00:22:37.220
has to be a subset.

00:22:37.220 --> 00:22:40.100
So a subspace is like a special
subset,

00:22:40.100 --> 00:22:43.850
but it's special for what
reason?

00:22:46.700 --> 00:22:49.700
You have to guess.

00:22:54.840 --> 00:22:56.580
Here's what it is.

00:22:56.580 --> 00:23:01.580
The main idea is that a subset
is special and called a subspace

00:23:01.580 --> 00:23:06.680
if it is closed under addition
and scalar multiplication.

00:23:07.140 --> 00:23:10.840
What that means is if you take
two elements in the subspace and

00:23:10.840 --> 00:23:13.100
take any linear combination of
them,

00:23:13.100 --> 00:23:15.900
the result is still within that
subspace.

00:23:15.900 --> 00:23:19.140
It's still in that smaller part
of the vector space.

00:23:19.140 --> 00:23:20.560
That is,

00:23:20.560 --> 00:23:25.540
if you take linear combinations
of vectors that live in here,

00:23:27.700 --> 00:23:31.640
those linear combinations all
also live in here.

00:23:31.740 --> 00:23:34.660
They don't escape out into the
bigger vector space.

00:23:34.660 --> 00:23:36.380
So it's like a little
neighborhood that you can't

00:23:36.380 --> 00:23:37.380
escape.

00:23:37.780 --> 00:23:42.600
The way that my sort of like
high school math club coach

00:23:42.600 --> 00:23:47.120
would put this was that if you
put two elephants in a cage and

00:23:47.120 --> 00:23:50.220
a third thing comes out, but it
has to be an elephant.

00:23:50.220 --> 00:23:52.300
That's all I think about this.

00:23:52.300 --> 00:23:56.260
If you put two vectors in a
subspace,

00:23:56.260 --> 00:23:57.360
OK, that's weird.

00:23:57.360 --> 00:23:59.360
Let's not say you put them in a
cage, but you take

00:23:59.360 --> 00:24:01.100
a linear combination of them,

00:24:01.100 --> 00:24:05.600
what you get back is another
thing in that subspace.

00:24:05.820 --> 00:24:08.380
So this should already sound
familiar to things that we've

00:24:08.380 --> 00:24:10.360
seen before.
We looked at the plane spanned

00:24:10.360 --> 00:24:12.120
by two vectors in R3,

00:24:12.120 --> 00:24:14.640
and we saw that any linear
combination of those vectors

00:24:14.640 --> 00:24:15.940
that spanned the plane

00:24:15.940 --> 00:24:18.500
also lived on the plane.
You can't escape the plane just

00:24:18.500 --> 00:24:20.320
using linear combinations.

00:24:20.320 --> 00:24:21.620
So this is how this is,

00:24:21.620 --> 00:24:24.000
like, we're formalizing things
we've already seen.

00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:25.900
So how might I write the
definition here?

00:24:25.900 --> 00:24:31.580
Suppose u and v are in s.

00:24:32.720 --> 00:24:37.800
What that means is their sum is
also in s.

00:24:37.800 --> 00:24:40.460
Of course, it means it's also in
v, but I'm saying,

00:24:40.460 --> 00:24:44.120
if you take two elements of this
little neighborhood and add

00:24:44.120 --> 00:24:44.620
them,

00:24:44.620 --> 00:24:47.340
the result is also in that
neighborhood.

00:24:47.340 --> 00:24:48.340
Similarly,

00:24:49.080 --> 00:24:54.280
if v is in S and c is a scalar,

00:24:54.280 --> 00:24:57.400
cv is also still in S.

00:24:57.400 --> 00:24:59.650
And then also,

00:25:00.320 --> 00:25:04.160
the zero vector needs to be in
S.

00:25:04.160 --> 00:25:06.780
Otherwise, scalar multiplication
wouldn't work, because then you

00:25:06.780 --> 00:25:10.530
wouldn't be able to multiply
elements by zero,

00:25:11.140 --> 00:25:16.520
OK? OK, so note that S itself is
its own vector space.

00:25:16.520 --> 00:25:21.500
These two lines that these
elements support addition and

00:25:21.500 --> 00:25:24.500
scalar multiplication tell you
that S is a vector space.

00:25:24.520 --> 00:25:26.680
You should think of it as a
vector space within another

00:25:26.680 --> 00:25:27.860
vector space.

00:25:27.860 --> 00:25:30.110
That is closed.

00:25:30.460 --> 00:25:32.710
That's the property.

00:25:48.300 --> 00:25:49.700
Now you might say,

00:25:49.700 --> 00:25:50.700
well,

00:25:51.300 --> 00:25:55.050
what subset wouldn't be a
subspace?

00:25:57.840 --> 00:25:59.960
Let's think of some examples.
And if you look at the extra

00:25:59.960 --> 00:26:02.440
practice exercises in
yesterday's lab, there were a

00:26:02.440 --> 00:26:03.440
few of these,

00:26:03.440 --> 00:26:08.600
but we didn't talk about this in
lecture, so maybe this was a

00:26:08.600 --> 00:26:10.900
little hard to think about.

00:26:10.900 --> 00:26:12.960
Let's suppose we're thinking of
R2.

00:26:12.960 --> 00:26:17.500
So our vector space V is R2.

00:26:17.500 --> 00:26:23.500
And let's say S is the set of
all vectors in R2

00:26:24.960 --> 00:26:29.160
where the x component is greater
than or equal to 0

00:26:29.160 --> 00:26:32.840
and the y component is greater
than or equal to 0.

00:26:32.840 --> 00:26:36.400
This is a perfectly valid subset
of vectors in R2.

00:26:36.400 --> 00:26:40.020
So I can make up any subset I
want.

00:26:40.020 --> 00:26:44.520
Visually, what does S here look
like?

00:26:47.600 --> 00:26:51.100
If I had to color this in and
try and depict what this

00:26:51.100 --> 00:26:53.980
collection of vectors looks
like, where would it live?

00:26:53.980 --> 00:26:57.060
Do you want to see possible
vectors in the first quadrant?

00:26:57.060 --> 00:27:00.120
Yeah, it's all of the vectors in
the top right quadrant,

00:27:00.120 --> 00:27:01.120
OK?

00:27:10.040 --> 00:27:13.360
So any vector whose endpoint
lives here, when I draw it from

00:27:13.360 --> 00:27:14.860
the origin,

00:27:15.360 --> 00:27:18.360
is in this collection.

00:27:22.740 --> 00:27:26.490
Is this collection a subspace?

00:27:27.260 --> 00:27:29.280
At first you might think, yeah,
sure, why not?

00:27:29.280 --> 00:27:31.780
If I take two vectors that live
in here,

00:27:31.780 --> 00:27:33.800
call this one v,

00:27:33.800 --> 00:27:35.900
call this one u,

00:27:35.900 --> 00:27:36.940
you add them up,

00:27:36.940 --> 00:27:38.200
yeah, sure.
u plus v is going to look

00:27:38.200 --> 00:27:40.450
something like that.

00:27:41.340 --> 00:27:43.680
If you start with two vectors
whose components are greater

00:27:43.680 --> 00:27:45.100
than or equal to 0,

00:27:45.100 --> 00:27:46.960
and you add the two vectors,

00:27:46.960 --> 00:27:49.340
the new vector's components will
also be greater than or equal to

00:27:49.340 --> 00:27:49.900
0.

00:27:49.900 --> 00:27:50.960
So addition is good.

00:27:50.960 --> 00:27:53.800
And the 0 vector is also
included, because I said x and y

00:27:53.800 --> 00:27:55.840
must be greater than or equal to
0.

00:27:55.840 --> 00:27:59.100
But what does this not satisfy?

00:27:59.100 --> 00:28:00.600
Hold on.
Can you multiply by negative?

